# Frontend UI Quality

- Follow the mandatory UI skill workflow in `AGENTS.md` before UI implementation, including its availability fallback and render verification requirements.

- Start with semantic HTML. Use real buttons and links for actions and navigation; preserve native keyboard behavior.
- Give every form control a visible label, every meaningful image useful alternative text, and every icon-only control an accessible name.
- Keep visible focus styles and logical tab order. Dialogs and overlays must manage focus and close with the keyboard where appropriate.
- Maintain at least 4.5:1 contrast for normal text, and never use color as the only status or validation signal.
- Make touch targets at least 44 × 44 CSS pixels and place validation feedback beside the affected control.
- Reuse the repository's color, typography, spacing, radius, shadow, and z-index tokens. Add a named token before repeating a new visual value.
- Use Lucide or an existing SVG icon set consistently. Use text or labeled icons instead of emoji as interface controls.
- Preserve space for images and asynchronous content to avoid layout shifts. Animate `transform` and `opacity` for micro-interactions, normally within 150–300 ms.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; the interface must remain understandable with motion disabled.
- Verify layouts at 375, 768, 1024, and 1440 pixels. Prevent horizontal scrolling and content hidden behind fixed elements.
- Keep localized product copy in its intended language while all technical names and comments follow the English naming rule.

Example: a clickable card uses a link or button, has a visible focus state, a stable hover treatment, and an accessible name.
